{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And it was, when the ark set out, etc. Arise, YHWH, etc.</b> One needs to know the intent of this saying. And the verse may be explained according to their words (Eitz Chaim, Gate 32, Chapter 1), which we have noted in several places, that the reason for Israel's journeying through the wilderness was to refine the holy sparks held captive in the domain of the dweller in the desert of Zin. And you must know that the external forces exist in two gradations: first, the aspect of the seducer and tempter, to cling to it to do evil and corrupt; second, the aspect of the damager and destroyer, whose nature is not to seduce. And the saying here comes — <b>And it was, when the ark set out</b> — for when it journeyed and all the holy sparks clung to it, and when the holy sparks were refined, the klipot shattered and broke. And this is the meaning of <b>Arise, YHWH</b>, <b>and let Your enemies be scattered</b> — these are the klipot that grasp hold of the holy sparks; they shall be scattered. And to them He alluded in the word <b>וַיְהִי</b>, in the sense of distress. And corresponding to the aspect of the evil that tempts man, He said <b>and let those who hate You flee from before You</b> — meaning, those aspects that make the way of YHWH hateful in the hearts of the children of man shall flee. And He used the plural form because they are many tempters, as they said, may their memory be for blessing, in Yoma (Yoma 69b), that there is an evil inclination for idolatry and for immorality, etc." "And all this was בִּנְסֹעַ — meaning, in the manner of journeying. But when he would encamp, he would say in a different manner, for HaShem, when He encamps in one place, thereby indicates that in the places where He did not encamp there was no refinement of holy sparks that would require encamping there, and passage through there would suffice. And in a place where he would encamp, it indicates that there are holy sparks there that require tarrying for their sake. And upon this Moses would intend and say upon resting <b>Return, YHWH, to the myriads of thousands of Israel</b> — meaning, that He should restore the holy sparks to gather all the myriads and thousands called Israel, for all holy sparks are called Israel. Or perhaps he alluded in the word <b>אַלְפֵי</b> in the sense of elevation and chieftainship, and you will find that the holy sparks which Samael the wicked captured were many and distinguished — go out and learn: from where did Ruth emerge? And Moses intended in this to pray for the strengthening of the power of Israel's holiness, with the aid of YHWH, to conquer and refine all that is found in that place."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {1 "ס״מ is an abbreviation for סַמָּאֵל (Samael). The phrase 'go out and learn: from where did Ruth emerge?' alludes to the rabbinic tradition (Bava Batra 91a) that Ruth descended from Moab, whose nation originated from sparks of holiness captured by the impure realm; 'go out and learn' (צֵא וּלְמַד) is a standard talmudic formula."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:07:33.553782529Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}